            <ArticleTitle>Isolated hepatic tuberculosis in the caudate lobe mimicking intrahepatic carcinoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e65-e67</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-7401(17)30035-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinre.2017.01.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although tuberculosis (TB) remains a worldwide epidemic, isolated hepatic TB is an extremely rare entity and poorly described in the literature. Isolated hepatic TB may pose a diagnostic dilemma due to the non-specific clinical symptoms and imaging features. We present a case of a 54-year-old female with an isolated caseating granulomatous lesion at the caudate lobe of liver involving the pancreatic head.</AbstractText>
